# Erdős Problem #99 — Target B explicit unique-max coherent phase gap

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Run type:** quantitative stability / explicit constant extraction  
**Target:** dense coherent three-phase global deficit-transfer gate  
**Depends on:** unique-max stability algebra; dual-tree dense-grid correction; trimmed-grid phase-star diameter theorem  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN

## 1. Statement

Let a coherent three-phase train component have phase classes

`A,B,C`

with sizes

`|A|=M`, `|B|=b`, `|C|=a`,

and suppose

`M>=b>=a`,

`a,b <= (1-kappa)M`,

`b >= tau M`,

for fixed constants `kappa,tau>0`.

Let `E` be the number of physical train crossings inside the coherent component and put

`Delta = [M(a+b)-E]/M^2`.

Assume the standard dense-core hypotheses used by the phase-stability reports:

1. the phase crossing graph has no transversal triangle;
2. the three phase directions are `o(1)`-coherent around the triangular projective directions;
3. crossings involving trains outside this coherent component are `o(M^2)`;
4. `T=O(M)`, so the component-scale Euler asymptotics apply.

Then

### Theorem 1 — explicit unique-max phase deficit

`liminf Delta >= 8*10^(-5) kappa tau`.

Thus the compactness-only `delta_phase>0` in the fixed-gap unique-max regime can be replaced by a concrete universal coefficient.

The constant is deliberately conservative. No numerical optimization is attempted.

## 2. Stability algebra converts phase deficit to one pairwise missing density

Write

`e_AB=Mb-x`,

`e_AC=Ma-y`,

`e_BC=z`.

The standard transversal-triple count gives

`Mz <= ax+by`.

Therefore

`M(a+b)-E=x+y-z`

`>= (1-a/M)x+(1-b/M)y`

`>= kappa(x+y)`.

In particular

`x <= Delta M^2/kappa`.

Let

`epsilon=x/(Mb)`

be the missing density of the `A-B` pair. Since `b>=tau M`,

`epsilon <= Delta/(kappa tau)`.                         (2.1)

Hence it is enough to prove that an admissible coherent component cannot satisfy

`epsilon < epsilon_0`,

where

`epsilon_0=8*10^(-5)`.                                 (2.2)

## 3. Quantitative dual-tree complete-grid extraction

The global-linear-stack claim used in the first phase-stability draft is false in general. Use instead the audited dual-tree correction.

Let `q=x=epsilon Mb` be the number of missing `A-B` crossings. Since `B` is internally noncrossing, its complement dual is a tree. Choose one train of `A` missing at most `epsilon b` tracks of `B`. Its crossing set is a dual-tree path `P_*` with at least

`(1-epsilon)b`

edges. This is the backbone.

Put

`r=sqrt(epsilon)`.

Call a train of `A` good if it misses at most `r b` tracks of the full family `B`. Markov gives at most

`epsilon M/r = rM`

bad trains. Thus there is a retained family

`A' subset A`,

`|A'| >= (1-r)M`.                                      (3.1)

The crossing set of every good `A` train intersects `P_*` in an interval. Since it misses at most `rb` backbone tracks, every such interval contains the central backbone obtained by deleting `rb` edges from each end. Hence there is

`B' subset B`,

`|B'| >= (1-epsilon-2r)b-O(1)`,                         (3.2)

such that every train in `A'` crosses every train in `B'`.

This is a complete topological grid.

Put

`p=|A'|/M`, `q_0=|B'|/M`, `t=b/M`, `s=a/M`.

Then asymptotically

`1-p <= r`,

`t-q_0 <= t(epsilon+2r) <= epsilon+2r`.

Define

`L=(1-p)+(t-q_0)`.

Therefore

`L <= 3r+epsilon+o(1)`.                                (3.3)

## 4. Uniform diameter margin of the complete phase star

Use the triangular phase vectors from the trimmed-grid theorem,

`u=(1,0)`,

`v=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2)`,

`w=(1/2,-sqrt(3)/2)`.

For the full ideal center/leaf sizes, define

`F(s,t)=min_(|xi|<=s)|u+t v+xi w|^2`.

The trimmed-grid theorem gives the exact formula

`F(s,t)=1+t+t^2-s(1-t)+s^2`

when `s<(1-t)/2`, and

`F(s,t)=3(1+t)^2/4`

when `s>=(1-t)/2`.

Let

`c=2sqrt(3)/pi`.

The proof of the trimmed-grid theorem contains a useful uniform numerical margin that was not isolated there:

### Lemma 2 — uniform complete-star margin

For every `0<=s<=t<=1`,

`F(s,t)-c(s+t) >= gamma_0`,

where

`gamma_0=3/4-c/2`

`=0.1986711045...`.                                    (4.1)

### Proof

In the constrained-minimizer branch `s>=(1-t)/2`, use `s<=t`:

`F-c(s+t) >= 3(1+t)^2/4-2ct`.

This quadratic has negative discriminant because `c<3/2`, and its minimum is larger than `gamma_0`.

In the other branch the expression decreases with `s`, so its minimum occurs at `s=(1-t)/2`, giving

`(1+t)[3(1+t)/4-c/2] >= 3/4-c/2=gamma_0`.

`square`

Also `F(s,t)<=3`, hence `sqrt(F)<=sqrt(3)`.

## 5. Cost of the grid deletions

The complete extracted grid uses only `A'` and `B'`. Relative to the full ideal vectors `u+t v`, shortening the two retained vector sums changes the normalized vector by at most `L` in Euclidean norm.

The omitted phase trains in `(A\A') union (B\B')` can themselves separate the selected grid diagonal and contribute with arbitrary sign. Their total normalized contribution has norm at most the same quantity `L`.

The entire coherent third phase contributes an arbitrary scalar multiple `xi w` with `|xi|<=s+o(1)`, exactly as already optimized in `F`. Truly external trains contribute only `o(M)` after the standard fixed-fraction central trim and the grid penetration lemma, because the non-core crossing mass is `o(M^2)`.

Consequently the selected source-point diagonal satisfies

`D/M >= sqrt(F(s,t))-2L-o(1)`.

Squaring and using `sqrt(F)<=sqrt(3)` gives the safe lower bound

`D^2/M^2 >= F(s,t)-4sqrt(3)L-o(1)`.                    (5.1)

No sign assumption on the omitted same-phase trains is used.

## 6. Insert the explicit missing-density threshold

For

`epsilon <= epsilon_0=8*10^(-5)`,

(3.3) gives

`L <= 3sqrt(epsilon_0)+epsilon_0+o(1)`

`<0.026913+o(1)`.

Hence

`4sqrt(3)L <0.1865+o(1)`.

By (4.1),

`F(s,t)-4sqrt(3)L`

`> c(s+t)+0.012+o(1)`.                                 (6.1)

Thus the extracted grid forces

`D^2 > [c(s+t)+0.01]M^2`

for all sufficiently large terms of the sequence.

On the other hand

`E=M(a+b)-Delta M^2`

and the coherent component carries all but `o(M^2)` rhombi, so

`R/M^2=s+t-Delta+o(1)`.

Since `T=O(M)`, Euler gives

`n/M^2=s+t-Delta+o(1)`.

The strict translated-triangular-lattice comparison therefore gives

`D^2/M^2 < c(s+t-Delta)+o(1)`

`<=c(s+t)+o(1)`,                                       (6.2)

contradicting (6.1).

Therefore `epsilon<epsilon_0` is impossible.

Combining with (2.1),

`Delta/(kappa tau) >= epsilon_0-o(1)`,

which proves Theorem 1.

## 7. Why the constant is useful

The earlier general coherent phase report proves only that a positive deficit exists after fixing a dense-core regime. Theorem 1 provides an explicit currency:

`M(a+b)-E >= 8*10^(-5) kappa tau M^2-o(M^2)`.

This can be inserted directly into a future global deficit-transfer inequality. The coefficient is tiny but is independent of the configuration and of the phase ratios once `kappa,tau` are fixed.

The remaining non-unique-max regime still needs its own explicit quantitative extraction. In particular the coefficient above degenerates as `kappa->0`, exactly where the two-largest-tie/balanced stability theorem takes over.

## 8. Audit cautions

- The false implication `pairwise noncrossing => complement dual is a path` is not used. The backbone order comes from one high-degree train in the dual tree.
- Omitted `A/B` phase trains are charged a second time as possible diagonal cancellation; they are not silently discarded.
- Truly external train error is controlled only after a further fixed-fraction trim of the already complete grid, followed by the asymptotic limit and then sending that trim fraction to zero.
- The theorem assumes the coherent component carries all but `o(M^2)` of the rhombus crossings at its scale, as in the existing phase endpoint reports.
- This is a local phase deficit theorem, not yet the global Target B contradiction.
